BRASS CAP IN CONCRETE ABUTMENT/PIER

-elevation 747.7m</groundspeak:encoded_hints>
      <groundspeak:logs>
        <groundspeak:log id="610436879">
          <groundspeak:date>2016-06-17T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="3999751">GJO_2010</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;BCP 417  Boulder Creek&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In Swan Hills area and with find #4600 being here, I wanted a classic virtual hide. With all the rain in the last 4 days, I figured the path into the Centre Of Alberta is probably a small stream right now so I went for this one (just recently found out about this great Alberta brass cap virtual). Quiet ride along the 33 to GZ, a small walk and found the cap(s) in great shape. Sent the info to the C.O., took a few pictures, then back on the road again. Thanks very much for keeping this virtual oldie going and dealing with hundreds of emails a week no doubt. A brave C.O.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="622373066">
          <groundspeak:date>2014-08-12T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="2395896">MKFmly</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP417 Boulder Creek &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Posted Coordinates: N55° 00.292 W115° 15.588

My GPSr Coordinates: N55° 00.294 W115° 15.591&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With a few days booked off we headed out on a caching and capping trip with a few objectives. Those objectives included having some fun and grabbing a few caches for various goals and challenges we have set for ourselves.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Wow it's been a long while since we found this cap. Just trying to get caught up again. It's always a pleasure to roam Alberta in search of the Brass.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;TFT Cap, outforthehunt!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="347938494">
          <groundspeak:date>2013-09-04T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="578246">BVPete</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP417 Bolder Creek

N55° 00.293’ W115° 15.585’&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Between Canyon Creek and this cap we experienced another hour delay due to assisting an “out of gas” female. I thought that only males interpreted “ E “ as meaning enough not empty &lt;img src="/images/icons/icon_smile_wink.gif" border="0" align="middle" /&gt;. We ended up driving her home, then we had to give her brother a boast to get his “car” started so they could go and retrieve her car. We left quickly then before we were assigned any more tasks &lt;img src="/images/icons/icon_smile_wink.gif" border="0" align="middle" /&gt;. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Finally, we headed south on the Grizzly Trail with our next stop being this cap. Boulder Creek is a very pretty spot in the middle of nowhere on this deserted hwy (at least today). The cap was an easy find and required no cleaning. The photo was taken and now it’s off to Whitecourt.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for posting this federal cap ofth, it sure helps break up that long drive&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="250820586">
          <groundspeak:date>2012-07-21T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="1835400">gordoner</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP417 - Boulder Creek &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Another find in a nice spot - this one was found on the way from Slave Lake down to Edmonton. TFTBC!!!!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="249461953">
          <groundspeak:date>2012-07-13T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="1565005">Takin it Easy</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP417 Boulder Creek

Posted: N55° 00.292 W115° 15.588

GPSr: N55° 00.292 W115° 15.588&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We were on our way from Slave Lake to Edmonton today to meet our oldest son at the Provincial Track Championships.  Our route passed by 5 caps of which this was the first.  This was an aptly named cap with the creek containing many boulders.  I quickly found the cap at the top of the bridge support as usual in these locations.  As I was taking the photo I noticed something on the side of the support within a foot or so of the first cap.  I think the second one is the correct cap as it is a federal benchmark.  Very cool!  Thanks for the cap OFTH!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
      </groundspeak:logs>
      <groundspeak:travelbugs />
